iovascular: Denies chest pain (other than current complaint), palpitations, edema.\X0A\Respiratory: Reports shortness of breath, denies cough, wheezing, sputum production.\X0A\Neurological: Denies headache, dizziness, syncope, focal weakness.\X0A\GI: Denies nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, abdominal pain.\X0A\GU: Denies dysuria, frequency, hematuria.\X0A\Musculoskeletal: Denies joint pain, muscle weakness.\X0A\\X0A\## Physical Examination\X0A\**General:** Alert and oriented, appears anxious, in mild respiratory distress.\X0A\**Vitals:** HR 110 bpm, BP 110/70 mmHg, SpO2 92% on room air, Temp 37.0°C (98.6°F).\X0A\**HEENT:** Atraumatic, normocephalic.\X0A\**Neck:** Supple, no JVD.\X0A\**Lungs:** Decreased breath sounds on the right side compared to the left. No wheezes or crackles auscultated bilaterally. Percussion reveals hyperresonance on the right side.\X0A\**Cardiovascular:** Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. No peripheral edema.\X0A\**Abdomen:** Soft, non-tender, non-distended. Bowel sounds present in all four quadrants.\X0A\**Neurological:** Grossly intact.\X0A\**Skin:** Warm, dry, no rashes or lesions.\X0A\\X0A\## Investigations\X0A\Chest X-ray ordered and reviewed. Findings show a right-sided pneumothorax.\X0A\\X0A\## Assessment\X0A\1.  **Right-sided Spontaneous Pneumothorax:** Based on the acute onset of pleuritic chest pain, shortness of breath, decreased breath sounds, hyperresonance to percussion on the right, and confirmed by chest X-ray.\X0A\\X0A\## Plan\X0A\1.  **Oxygen:** Apply supplemental oxygen via nasal cannula to maintain SpO2 > 94%.\X0A\2.  **Pain Management:** Administer IV morphine or fentanyl for pain control.\X0A\3.  **Chest Tube Placement:** Consult Thoracic Surgery for placement of a right-sided chest tube (likely a small-bore catheter) for drainage of air and lung re-expansion.\X0A\4.  **Monitoring:** Continuous cardiac monitoring and frequent reassessment of respiratory status and pain level.\X0A\5.  **Smoking Cessation Counseling:** Provide education and resources for smoking cessation.\X0A\6.  **Discharge Planning:** Discuss potential need for pleurodesis or surgical intervention if recurrent pneumothorax occurs. Educate patient on signs and symptoms of pneumothorax recurrence.\X0A\